    explanation: "What you eat carries a carbon cost, and animal products — especially red meat — dominate the food footprint because raising livestock consumes land, feed, and energy while cattle emit methane. This diet carbon footprint calculator estimates the yearly CO₂ from three high-impact foods: beef, chicken, and dairy. Enter your typical weekly consumption of each and it returns your annual emissions in kilograms of CO₂-equivalent.\n\nThe factors reveal the stark differences between foods. Beef emits roughly 27 kilograms of CO₂-equivalent per kilogram, chicken about 6.9, and dairy around 3.2, so a kilogram of beef carries several times the footprint of the same weight of poultry. This is why shifting even part of your red-meat consumption toward chicken, or toward plant proteins, cuts your dietary emissions sharply.\n\nSeeing the annual figure makes food choices concrete alongside driving and home energy. Many people are surprised that a heavy meat diet can rival a car or a flight in its yearly carbon impact. The calculator is not about eliminating foods but about understanding trade-offs so you can decide where changes feel worthwhile. People exploring lower-carbon diets, students studying food systems, and households setting sustainability goals use a diet carbon footprint calculator to quantify the effect of what is on their plate and to target the swaps that reduce the most carbon for the least disruption.",
    example: { inputs: { beefKgPerWeek: 1, chickenKgPerWeek: 2, dairyKgPerWeek: 3 }, explanation: "(1 × 27 + 2 × 6.9 + 3 × 3.2) × 52 = (27 + 13.8 + 9.6) × 52 = 50.4 × 52 ≈ 2,621 kg of CO₂ a year." },
